    "title": "Compact object",
    "pillar": "science",
    "explanation": "A compact object in astronomy is a very dense stellar remnant such as a white dwarf, neutron star, or black hole; hypothetical exotic stars could also count. These objects pack a lot of mass into a small radius, so their density is far higher than ordinary matter. The term is used when a strong gravitational effect is found in a small region but the object's exact nature is unclear.\n\nMost compact objects are endpoints of stellar evolution, often called stellar remnants or “dead stars”; which type forms depends mainly on the progenitor star's mass. Compact objects that aren't black holes are often called degenerate stars. Some energetic phenomena—like certain fast radio bursts—may arise from compact-object mergers or from magnetars, a kind of neutron star.",
